/* data/learn-c/cs-12-02.js - GENERATED by tools/build_learn.py from data/learn/summaries/cs-12-02.json (our own words;
 * the notes of one chapter, loaded when the chapter is opened). Do not edit by hand. */
(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["cs-12-02"] = {"1":{"s":"Data are raw facts; information is processed data. Data items are grouped into records and files. A record is a group of related fields; a primary key is a field whose value is different in every record. A data structure is a way of arranging data in memory so that it can be used efficiently. Examples are arrays, linked lists, stacks, queues and trees.","w":["A group item divides into subitems (a name into first and last name); an elementary item does not (a pin code).","A record can hold fields of different types; an array holds elements of the same type."],"x":["Data are raw facts such as numbers and words. When data is processed and has meaning, it is information. Data items can be elementary (not divided, like a roll number) or group items (divided into subitems, like a name or a date).","A record is a collection of related fields about one thing, such as the roll number, name and marks of a student. A file is a collection of related records. The field whose value is different for every record, like the roll number, is the primary key and it finds exactly one record.","A data structure is the way data is arranged and kept in memory, together with the operations on it. The choice of the data structure decides how fast a program runs and how much memory it needs. Linear structures (array, linked list, stack, queue) keep items in a line; a tree is non-linear."],"e":"A school file of 500 students has 500 records. Each record has the fields roll number (the primary key), name and marks."},"2":{"s":"The operations on a data structure are traversing (visit every element once), searching (find the place of an element), inserting (add an element), deleting (remove an element), sorting (arrange in ascending or descending order) and merging (join two sorted lists). Which structure is best depends on which operation is done most often.","w":["Traversing visits every element once; searching stops when the element is found.","Merging joins two lists that are already sorted into one sorted list."],"x":["Traversing means going through the elements one by one and processing each exactly once, for example to print a list. Searching means finding where an item is kept; the search is successful if the item is found and unsuccessful if it is not.","Inserting adds a new element at a given place; deleting removes an element and closes the gap. Sorting arranges the elements in order, which makes searching faster. Merging joins two sorted lists into one sorted list.","Each operation costs time. An array is fast to reach by position but slow to insert in the middle (elements must be moved). A linked list is quick to insert and delete (only pointers change) but slow to reach by position."],"e":"To find a name in a class list you search; to add a new student you insert; to print all names you traverse; to arrange them A to Z you sort."},"3":{"s":"An algorithm is a finite list of clear steps that solves a problem. It is written in steps with a name, the inputs and the output, and each step is simple and exact. Algorithmic notation uses words like Set, Read, Print, Repeat while and If. An algorithm must end after a limited number of steps.","w":["An algorithm is written before the program and does not depend on a language.","Every algorithm must end; a loop whose condition never becomes false is a mistake."],"x":["An algorithm is a step by step method to solve a problem. It must be finite (it ends), definite (each step is clear), and it must give the answer for the given input. The board writes an algorithm as numbered steps, with a name, the data and the variables used.","Usual words: Set to give a value (Set K = 1), Read and Print for input and output, Repeat steps while a condition is true, If and else for a decision, and Exit to end. Steps that depend on a loop or a condition are written under it.","An algorithm is not a program. It can be turned into a program in C++ or any language. Writing it first helps to find mistakes early."],"e":"Algorithm to find the larger of two numbers A and B: Step 1 Read A and B. Step 2 If A is greater than B, print A, else print B. Step 3 Exit."},"4":{"s":"Control structures decide the order in which the steps of an algorithm are done. There are three: sequence (steps one after another), selection (the conditional structure: If then, If then else, to choose between steps) and iteration (the repeat structure: Repeat while a condition is true, or Repeat for a counter).","w":["The repeat while structure tests its condition before each round.","In If then else only one of the two modules is done."],"x":["Sequence: the steps are done one after another in the order written. Selection: the conditional structure chooses what to do. The If then form does a module only when a condition is true; the If then else form does one module when the condition is true and another when it is false.","Iteration: the repeat structure does a set of steps again and again. In Repeat while the steps are done as long as the condition is true, and the condition is tested first. In the Repeat for form a counter goes from a start value to an end value.","In a loop something must change the condition, otherwise it never ends. Most algorithms mix the three structures."],"e":"Print the numbers 1 to 5: Set K = 1. Repeat while K is at most 5: print K, set K = K + 1. Exit."},"5":{"s":"An array is a linear list of a fixed number of elements of the same type, stored in consecutive memory locations and reached by an index. The address of an element is Base + w times (K minus lower bound), where w is the size of one element. Operations: traversing, inserting, deleting, sorting (bubble sort) and searching (linear and binary search). Pointer arrays and records in memory are also studied.","w":["Binary search works only on a sorted array; linear search works on any array.","Inserting or deleting in the middle of an array needs the elements to be moved."],"x":["An array keeps elements of one type in consecutive locations under one name, and each is reached by its subscript, A[1], A[2] and so on. The computer remembers only the base address (the address of the first element). The address of the Kth element is Base(A) + w x (K - LB), where w is the size of one element and LB is the lower bound.","Traversing goes from the lower to the upper bound. Insertion at position K moves the elements from K onwards one place to the right, starting from the last, and then puts the new item at K. Deletion at position K moves the later elements one place to the left. Bubble sort compares neighbours and swaps those that are out of order; after each pass the largest remaining element is at the end.","Linear search compares the item with each element from the start. Binary search needs a sorted array: it compares the item with the middle element and throws away half of the array each time, so it needs far fewer comparisons.","A pointer variable holds an address; a pointer array is an array of pointers, useful for long records of different lengths. Records can be kept in memory as consecutive fields, and many records as an array of records."],"e":"Sorted array 10, 20, 30, 40, 50. To find 40 by binary search: the middle is 30; 40 is bigger so keep the right half; the middle of 40, 50 is 40; found in 2 comparisons."},"6":{"s":"A linked list is a linear collection of nodes. Each node has data and a pointer to the next node; the last has a null pointer and a START variable holds the first node. In memory it uses parallel arrays INFO and LINK and a free list AVAIL. Its size can change while the program runs, and insertion and deletion only change pointers, so they are easy. It cannot be reached by position directly.","w":["A linked list node holds data and a pointer; an array element holds only data.","To reach the nth node you must follow the links from START; there is no direct access."],"x":["A linked list is a chain of nodes. Each node has two parts: the information and the pointer (link) to the next node. A pointer START keeps the address of the first node, and the last node has a null pointer to show the end.","In memory the list is kept in two parallel arrays: INFO holds the data and LINK holds the location of the next node. The unused nodes are kept together in a free storage list called AVAIL. When a node is inserted it is taken from AVAIL; when a node is deleted it is returned to AVAIL.","Advantages over an array: the size is not fixed, memory is used as needed, and insertion and deletion need no moving of elements, only a change of pointers. Disadvantages: it needs extra memory for the pointers and it has no direct access to the nth element."],"e":"To insert a node between nodes A and B, take a node from AVAIL, put the data in it, set its link to B, and change the link of A to the new node."},"7":{"s":"A tree is a non-linear data structure with one root node and other nodes below it joined by branches; a node without children is a leaf. A binary tree has at most two children for each node, the left child and the right child. An extended 2-tree has 0 or 2 children for every node. An expression can be drawn as a binary tree. In memory a binary tree uses arrays INFO, LEFT, RIGHT and ROOT, or one array.","w":["A binary tree can be empty and every node has at most two children; a general tree can have more.","In the sequential form the node at position K has its children at 2K and 2K plus 1."],"x":["A tree shows a hierarchy such as a family tree. The top node is the root. Each node except the root has one parent. Nodes without children are leaves. The depth (height) is the number of levels.","A binary tree has at most two children for each node, called the left child and the right child. An extended 2-tree is made from a binary tree by adding special external nodes where a child is missing, so that every node has 0 or 2 children. The original nodes are internal nodes.","An algebraic expression is drawn as a binary tree with the operators in the internal nodes and the operands in the leaves. The operator of the last operation done is the root.","In memory a binary tree is kept in the linked form with three parallel arrays: INFO for the data, LEFT and RIGHT for the locations of the children, and ROOT for the root. In the sequential form the whole tree is in one array: the root is at position 1, the children of the node at K are at 2K and 2K plus 1, and empty places are left blank."],"e":"The expression A plus B times C: the root is the plus operator, its left child is A, and its right child is the times operator whose children are B and C."}};
